---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="Projects — Alexander Mervar" description="Projects and work by Alexander Mervar">
  <h1>Projects</h1>

  <h2>Sites</h2>
  <ul class="post-list">
    <li>
      <p class="post-title">
        <a href="https://oscars.alexandermervar.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Oscars Party</a>
      </p>
      <p class="post-description">A ballot and viewing guide I build each year for an Oscars party. Guests fill out their predictions before the ceremony and we score them live. Rebuilt annually as the nominees change.</p>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Hardware &amp; Tools</h2>
  <ul class="post-list">
    <li>
      <p class="post-title">
        <a href="https://github.com/alexandermervar/hal-1000" target="_blank" rel="noopener">HAL-1000</a>
      </p>
      <p class="post-description">A Raspberry Pi project. Named after the fictional AI from <em>2001: A Space Odyssey</em>.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p class="post-title">
        <a href="https://github.com/alexandermervar/PIEthon-2.0" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PIEthon 2.0</a>
      </p>
      <p class="post-description">A Python tool for pulling reports and exporting datasets out of Indiana University's Pie TCC platform. A full rewrite of an earlier version originally built by Brian Funk at TCC UITS.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p class="post-title">
        <a href="https://github.com/alexandermervar/automatedCSSetup" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Automated CS Setup</a>
      </p>
      <p class="post-description">A Python script that automates the process of opening the specific set of web tools used by UITS staff at Indiana University's TCC. Cuts down on repetitive startup steps at the beginning of each shift.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p class="post-title">
        <a href="https://github.com/alexandermervar/C211-Grading-Algorithm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">C211 Grading Algorithm</a>
      </p>
      <p class="post-description">A small Jupyter notebook for grading student code submissions in CSCI-C211 (Introduction to Computer Science) at Indiana University. Applies a consistent rubric so scores don't drift across a large batch of submissions.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p class="post-title">
        <a href="https://github.com/alexandermervar/optimalstop" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Optimal Stop</a>
      </p>
      <p class="post-description">A Python implementation of the optimal stopping problem — the mathematical strategy for knowing when to commit to a choice after evaluating options in sequence. Also known as the secretary problem.</p>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Coursework</h2>
  <p>Academic work from Indiana University, kept public for reference.</p>
  <ul class="post-list">
    <li>
      <p class="post-title">
        <a href="https://github.com/alexandermervar/cogs-q320" target="_blank" rel="noopener">COGS-Q 320 — Computation in Cognitive Science</a>
      </p>
      <p class="post-description">Notebooks and assignments exploring how computational models are used to study cognition. Covers topics like simulation, data analysis, and cognitive architectures using Python and Jupyter.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p class="post-title">
        <a href="https://github.com/alexandermervar/psychopy-tutorial" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PsychoPy Tutorial</a>
      </p>
      <p class="post-description">A tutorial built for COGS-Q 320 introducing PsychoPy, a Python library for running psychology experiments. Covers stimulus presentation, response collection, and timing.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p class="post-title">
        <a href="https://github.com/alexandermervar/cogs-q351" target="_blank" rel="noopener">COGS-Q 351</a>
